so a bare layout panel no longer swallows clicks meant for what is behind it.

member.summary:
Constructs a component, containing anything its constructor throws. A field initializer is
compiled into the constructor, so user code runs here and can fail for any reason at all;
letting that escape would take down whichever menu, drop target or scene load asked for it.

A post effect written as one fragment shader, the simplest kind there is. Each 
field reaches the shader as a uniform named after it with an underscore, Amount as _Amount: floats, ints and
enums as themselves, bools as a float 0 or 1, colours and Float2 to Float4 as vectors, Texture2D as a sampler2D.
The shader reads the colour with Graphite_SampleSource(vUV) and writes outColor.

[VolumeComponentMenu("Custom/Invert")]
public sealed class Invert : PostEffect
{
    public VolumeParameter<float> Amount = new(0f);
    public override bool IsActive => Amount.Value > 0f;
    protected override string Shader => """
        uniform float _Amount;
        void main()
        {
            vec4 c = Graphite_SampleSource(vUV);
            outColor = vec4(mix(c.rgb, 1.0 - c.rgb, _Amount), c.a);
        }
        """;
}

Effects needing several passes or textures of their own derive from  instead.

Ear-clipping triangulation of a simple polygon. Appends triangle indices (into )
to  and returns false if the polygon is degenerate. Holes aren't handled;
a hole loop just triangulates as a solid, which is harmless since its texels are transparent.

member.remarks:
Created by  during asset
import with the project's SDF settings. Treat as read-only, changing those settings reimports every mesh.

Volume sampling (GPU shader):
  uvw = (localPos - Bounds.Min) / (Bounds.Max - Bounds.Min);
  distance = texture(volume, uvw).r * MaxDistance;

The scene belongs to the main thread. Worker threads are for pure computation: read what you need on
the main thread, compute on a worker, then come back to apply the result.

var input = BuildInput();
await GameTask.WorkerThread();
var result = Compute(input);
await GameTask.MainThread();
Apply(result);

Awaiting in a component resumes on the main thread already. When play stops or scripts reload, the
session ends:  is cancelled and pending continuations are dropped, including a
worker's hop back to the main thread when the work was started by gameplay code. Frame waits from an ended
session complete as cancelled. Once the loop stops, requests for the main thread are refused.


When no engine loop is running, such as in a test or a tool, every thread counts as the main thread.

member.summary:
Connects to the headset through the active OpenXR runtime. Returns false, and logs why, when there is no
runtime, no headset, the runtime cannot render with Vulkan, or the device was not made for XR (see
).

